.TH XUR.STD.STR 7 "2026-10-27" "xur 0.1.1" "Xur Manual"
.SH NAME
xur.std.str \- string methods
.SH DESCRIPTION
These are methods on string values. They are not imported from a module;
they are available on any
.B str
value directly.
.SH METHODS
.TP
.B len() -> i64
Number of characters (Unicode scalar values).
.TP
.B is_empty() -> bool
Whether the string is empty.
.TP
.B to_upper() -> str
Convert to uppercase.
.TP
.B to_lower() -> str
Convert to lowercase.
.TP
.B trim() -> str
Remove leading and trailing whitespace.
.TP
.B contains(part) -> bool
Whether the string contains
.IR part .
.TP
.B starts_with(prefix) -> bool
Whether the string starts with
.IR prefix .
.TP
.B ends_with(suffix) -> bool
Whether the string ends with
.IR suffix .
.TP
.B replace(from, to) -> str
Replace all occurrences of
.I from
with
.IR to .
.TP
.B split(separator) -> [str]
Split the string by
.I separator
into a list.
.TP
.B lines() -> [str]
Split the string into lines.
.TP
.B repeat(n) -> str
Repeat the string
.I n
times.
.TP
.B pad_left(width, fill) -> str
Pad the string on the left with
.I fill
characters until it reaches
.I width
characters.
.TP
.B parse_int() -> Result<i64, str>
Parse the string as an integer.
.TP
.B parse_float() -> Result<f64, str>
Parse the string as a float.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
use std::io;

fn main() {
    let s = "  Hello, World!  ";
    io::println("{}", s.trim());
    io::println("{}", s.trim().to_upper());
    io::println("{}", "a,b,c".split(",").join(" | "));
    io::println("{}", "42".parse_int().unwrap());
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xur.std (7)
